Thread VP9 software decoding by rows on OHOS, and say how long frames take.

A phone here decodes 10-bit VP9 in software: it has no VP9 hardware decoder,
and unlike mobile Android this port builds libvpx with high bit depth, so
YouTube serves it 1080p60 HDR (itag 335). On a Mate 70 Pro+ one such video
dropped 1% of its frames and another 30%, with the decode thread about half
busy on both and libvpx's other threads idle: libvpx threads across tile
columns only, and a frame that ran long on the one busy thread missed its
16.7 ms and was dropped.

On OHOS the VP9 decoder asks libvpx to thread by rows as well
(VP9D_SET_ROW_MT), which spreads every frame over the decoder's threads
whatever its tiles, and logs its thread count once and, every 300 frames,
the average and longest vpx_codec_decode time and how many frames went over
16.7 ms ("OHOS vpx decode").

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-vp9-row-threads.patch

